    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "Shinto, the ever-nebulous religion of Japan, has no settled doctrine or origin story. At its simplest, Shinto beliefs gravitate towards a fluid idea of kami. Kami are the personified concepts of wind, rivers, trees, and other natural elements. Due to the influence of Christianity, the concept of an afterlife was introduced, and some followers believe humans become kami after death.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0007",
        "chunk_title": "5. Shinto",
        "chunk_order": 6,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "historical_context",
      "value": "The religion became more concrete in the events surrounding WWII, wherein the Japanese government instituted it as a state religion that aimed to venerate the emperor as a living, human kami.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0007",
        "chunk_title": "5. Shinto",
        "chunk_order": 6,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "development",
      "value": "Shinto beliefs have developed since the 6th century as a nature-focused series of scattered beliefs that merged and then split with Buddhism as well as Confucianism.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0007",
        "chunk_title": "5. Shinto",
